module j3.data.pool
    Typed handles `Id<T>` into a `Pool<T>`.
    A `Pool<T>` stores values in a `List<T>` and hands out `Id<T>`, a `u32` index that only fits pools of the
    same `T` (`Id<Expr>` passed where `Id<Stmt>` is wanted is a type error). Ids are never reused (there is no
    remove), compare with `==`, and hash, so they work as `j3.data.map.Map` keys. The index-based IR style:
    `let e = exprs.add(Expr.Num(1)); exprs.get(e)`.

struct Id<T>
    Index of a value in a `Pool<T>`. `T` only tags the type.
    i: u32
    fn Id<T>.operator==(this: Id<T>, o: Id<T>): bool
    fn Id<T>.hash(this: Id<T>): u64

struct Pool<T>
    Values of one type, addressed by `Id<T>`.
    items: List<T>
    fn Pool<T>.new(arena: &Arena): Pool<T>
        Empty pool allocating from `arena`.
    fn Pool<T>.add(this: &Pool<T>, v: T): Id<T>
        Store `v` and return its handle.
    fn Pool<T>.get(this: &Pool<T>, id: Id<T>): &T
        Pointer to the value of `id`, valid until the next `add` (growth moves the storage). Panics on an id
        from another pool that is out of range here.
    fn Pool<T>.len(this: &Pool<T>): int
        Number of values.
    fn Pool<T>.idAt(this: &Pool<T>, i: int): Id<T>
        The handle of the `i`th value (0..len), for walking every entry.

